          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Where generally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            is an invariance under a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            (after all the mathematical term “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"}> group
            ” is a contraction of
            = succeed ")," do
              %em group of symmetries
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            action, in the general sense of the word, a
            %strong supersymmetry
            is invariance under a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergroup"} supergroup
            action or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            action.
          %p
            By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne%27s+theorem+on+tensor+categories"} Deligne's theorem on tensor categories
            it is precisely the context of supersymmetry in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+categories"} tensor categories
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            exhibit full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
          %p
            In the stricter sense of the word as it originates in theoretical fundamental
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %em supersymmetry
            refers specifically to supergeometric extensions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry+group"} isometry group
            (or its Lie algebra) of the local model of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} Poincaré Lie algebra
            and their super-extensions are accordingly known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %strong the supersymmetry algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            is called a
            %strong supersymmetry generator
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercharge"} supercharge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+geometry"} conformal geometry
            or
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+spacetime"} de Sitter spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti-de Sitter spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+group"} conformal group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+group"} de Sitter group
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+group"} anti de Sitter group
          %p
            With the focus on spacetime symmetry groups implicitly understood, this implies that supersymmetry is the group theory relevant for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartan+geometry"} super Cartan geometry
            locally modeled on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetime"} super Minkowski spacetime
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} super anti de Sitter spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            or, respectively, from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconformal+field+theory"} superconformal field theory
          %h3#global_supersymmetry_and_superparticles Global supersymmetry and superparticles
          %p
            By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner-Weyl+theory"} Wigner-Weyl theory
            we have in ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representations+of+the+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} unitary representations of the Poincaré group
            correspond to the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            %em globally
            supersymmetric quantum field theory the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            here is replaced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} super Poincaré group
            and accordingly particles are now
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible representation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representations+of+the+super+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} unitary representations of the super Poincaré group
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermultiplets"} supermultiplets
            – appearing this way are called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpartners"} superpartners
            of the original
            %em bosonic
            particles.
          %h3#local_supersymmetry_supergravity Local supersymmetry: supergravity
          %p
            To be distinguished from this
            %em global supersymmetry
            is
            %em local supersymmetry
            (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            and at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            whose fields are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            with values in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} Poincaré Lie algebra
            – the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation
            – a supersymmetric extension is similarly a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            with fields being connections in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            – a theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            in such a theory is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong local supersymmetry transformation
          %h3#the_relation_between_local_and_global_supersymmetry The relation between local and global supersymmetry
          %p
            The distinction between local and global supersymmetry is important when considering
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            where all fields are expanded around a fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            and fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+fields"} background gauge fields
            that form a solution of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
          %p
            While the theory of supergravity as such has, by definition, local supersymmetry, a solution to it may but need not have any
            %em global supersymmetry
            left. In fact, generically it will not.
          %p
            To see this, it is maybe helpful to compare with the analogous statement in non-supersymmetric
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
          %p
            the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is
            %em locally
            Poincaré-symmetric: in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation
            it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
            – may, but need not, have any Poincaré-symmetry. It does have such a
            %em global
            symmetry for every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector"} Killing vector
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            The analog of these Killing vectors in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            are
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariantly+constant+spinor"} covariantly constant spinor
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
            annihilated by the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
          %p
            The most famous example of this is that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} Calabi-Yau manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+building"} model building
            that used to be very popular around the turn of the millennium (but is maybe experimentally ruled out at the time of this writing) is that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                we see around us locally looks like a product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 4
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 6
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4 \times Y^6
                of 4-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
                times a tiny closed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 6
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
                (so tiny that it is not directly observable but manifests itself only by way of its lowest excitation modes that look like different
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
                species on the remaining
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4
                – see
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
                );
            %li
              %p
                such that on this product space a single covariant constant spinor exists, such that the resulting effective theory on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4
                has a single global supersymmetry (“
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=1
                supersymmetry”).
          %p
            One finds that this condition is solved precisely by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
            being a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
          %p
            But notice that nothing in the theory of 10-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %em demands
            that its solutions have a global supersymmetry left (generically they will not) nor that they factor locally as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi M
                      %mn 4
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4 \times Y^6
            %em ansatz
            a
            %em phenomenological model
            \. It only says that
            %em if
            we make this ansatz, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
            needs to be a Calabi-Yau space. In fact, it turns out to be nontrivial to check that with all the other fields taken into account, such a factor ansatz is indeed consistent. (This problem of “moduli stabilization” is discussed a little bit at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %p
            Latest experimental results strongly suggest that this model of global
            = succeed "-supersymmetry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (N = 1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            but
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %h3#observed_supersymmetry_on_the_worldline Observed supersymmetry: on the worldline
          %p
            To appreciate this point it is worthwhile to notice that supersymmetric quantum field theory in fact
            %em has
            been observed in nature, already since a century ago. To see this, one needs to notice how
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            quantum field theories (see there) on their
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
          %p
            the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            particle – such as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+supersymmetry"} worldline supersymmetry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
            \. Notice that this is true for electrons and quarks in the non-supersymmetric
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            theory is completely void of (global) supersymmetry, and still the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            theory of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            is automatically supersymmetric.
          %h3#SupersymmetryOnSpacetimeAndWorldsheet Conjectured supersymmetry: on spacetime and on the worldsheet
          %p
            A similar statement as for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
            and its automatic local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+supersymmetry"} worldline supersymmetry
            indeed also holds for the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"} spinning string
            \: while it is a conjecture of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            which has not been experimentally verified that fundamentally the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            theories that describe the physics around us is defined not on 1-dimensional but on 2-dimensional
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            %em
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            just happens to be locally supersymmetric. It is hard to avoid this! And indeed, this was how the abstract notion of supersymmetry in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            was realized in the west first: people wrote down action functionals for
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"}> spinning string
              s
            and noticed that these happened to have an interesting graded symmetry (see there for references).
          %p
            So while it is an open conjecture of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            that the particle
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
          %p
            Now, the effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            theories arising from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            are fairly well understood: these are higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories coupled to the various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} higher gauge fields
            that are also in the spectrum of the string (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            and the
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            This means that the assumption of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"} spinning string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
            automatically implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            that we observe also has
            %em local
            supersymmetry.
          %p
            Over the decades it has often been suggested that therefore the assumption of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"} spinning string
          %p
            in these constructions the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            species seen in accelerators are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} KK-modes
            and/or brane-brane open string modes of the compactified locally supersymmetric theory. This means that they are determined by the compactification geometry. Only if that has a global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            is the effective 4-dimensional theory globally supersymmetric and exhibits superpartners. As was mentioned above, for spacetimes of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^4 \times Y^6
            this is the case precisely if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^6
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
          %p
            But this is far from being the generic situation. This is clear qualitatively (a generic solution to the super-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            will not have a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor"} Killing spinor
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DLSW"} DLSW08
          %h3#in_fundamental_physics In fundamental physics
          %p
            Supersymmetric extensions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            have been felt to be compelling in fundamental physics for
            %em formal reasons
            \: the simple step from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                make a pure theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                seamlessly incorporate
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %li
              %p
                leads to better
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
                properties (indeed the speculation that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mn 8
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=8, D=4
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                is fully renormalizable – in stark contrast to ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                has more recently been checked up to high loop orders);
            %li
              %p produces a wealth of interesting mathematical structures. For instance
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse+theory"} Morse theory
                    of a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
                    can naturally be understood in terms of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
                    for
                    = succeed "s" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superparticle"} superparticle
                %li
                  %p
                    the interpretation of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
                    in terms of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
                    theories only works for supersymmetric theories (see
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
                    and
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theory"} (2,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theory
                %li
                  %p
                    “topological twists” of supersymmetric field theories are a major source of examples of
                    = succeed "s," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
                    and the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
                    arise from such twists of 2-dimensional
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                    and there are deep connections between the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
                    and topologically twisted
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Moreover, since various observables in supersymmetric QFTs are easier to compute than in non-supersymmetric theories, supersymmetric quantum field theory is being used to
            %em approximate
            certain aspects of other QFTs. For instance certain correlators in ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors+in+Yang-Mills+theory"} spinors in Yang-Mills theory
            can be computed using an auxiliary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
          %p Therefore, if nothing else, supersymmetric quantum field theories constitute a part of the whole space of quantum field theories which is useful for understanding general properties of that space. What is however still missing is any experimental evidence that the world is fundamentally described by a supersymmetric quantum field theory.
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p We start by saying what it means, in generality, to have a supersymmetric extension of an ordinary symmetry, then we specialize to spacetime supersymmetry.
          %p
            Here we are concerned with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+groups"} symmetry groups
            that are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
          %p
            To discuss super-extensions of Lie algebras, recall from
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supergeometry"} geometry of physics – supergeometry
            the concept of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebras"} super Lie algebras
          #SuperLieAlgebraAsLieAlgebraInternalToSuperVectorSpaces.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sVect
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi τ
                      %mi super
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sVect = (Vect^{\mathbb{Z}/2}, \otimes_k, \tau^{super} )
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+spaces"} super vector spaces
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+space"} super vector space
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝔤
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              %li
                %p a homomorphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi 𝔤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        [-,-] \;\colon\; \mathfrak{g} \otimes_k \mathfrak{g} \longrightarrow \mathfrak{g}
                %p
                  of super vector spaces (the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em super Lie bracket
            %p such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
