                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 7
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_3 = \mathfrak{so}(7)
              in its even parts acting spinorially on its odd part. This hence gives a superconformal super Lie algebra in dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 7
                      %mo −
                      %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 7-2 = 5
            %p
              But other spinor representations may still disguise as vector representations of other Lie algebras under one of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "spin%20group#ExceptionalIsomorphisms"} exceptional isomorphisms
            %p First there is the exceptional isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔰𝔬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 5
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
